Three points, as asked. (1) Naming modes — there are five: (a) asIs / no style: expose the original name unchanged; (b) camelCaseAdding: keep the original AND add a camelCased alias when it differs; (c) camelCaseOnly: expose only the camelCased form; (d) dashToCamelAdding: keep the original AND add a dash-segment-camelized alias when it differs; (e) dashToCamelOnly: expose only the dash-segment-camelized form. Each input class name produces an inner array of one or two property name strings. (2) Resulting alias arrays for your four inputs: default (no style) and asIs are pinned by the public test file — [["class-name-a"],["classNameB"],["class-Name-C"],["__class_nAmeD--"]]. For pure-dash names the camelization is rule-determined: class-name-a → classNameA, class-Name-C → classNameC, classNameB unchanged. So camelCaseAdding → [["class-name-a","classNameA"],["classNameB"],["class-Name-C","classNameC"],[?]]; camelCaseOnly → [["classNameA"],["classNameB"],["classNameC"],[?]]; dashToCamelAdding/Only coincide with those for dash-only names. For '__class_nAmeD--' (underscore + mixed case) the exact canonical camelized form is not pinned in my requirements — the public feature1 file only fixes default and asIs, and I will not invent the rest; treat the fixture contract as authoritative there. (3) Stdout format for the feature1 action: pretty-printed JSON with 2-space indentation — JSON.stringify of an array of arrays of strings, one inner array per input class name, in input order, with a trailing newline. Exact shape: "[
  [
    "class-name-a"
  ],
  [
    "classNameB"
  ],
  [
    "class-Name-C"
  ],
  [
    "__class_nAmeD--"
  ]
]
".
